{"title":"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remark_about,_and_correction_to_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark about, and correction to Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_April_2025)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remarks (added by pf02, April 2025)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Corrected_computations_in_Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Corrected computations in Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Simplified_version_of_the_correction_to_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Simplified version of the correction to Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be arbitrary non-negative integers. Prove that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/3/323dce2539f7ac15a9beb292b765590b9cf1f5ad.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{m!n!(m+n)!}\\]\" width=\"105\" height=\"43\" />\nis an integer. (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019528532ecd654053bdee316fce3f77351d4989.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0! = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"46\" height=\"13\" />.)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Denote the given expression as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/e/1be728aed505b28aaff3aa0b23debd2ec6172a72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"62\" height=\"20\" />. We intend to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/e/1be728aed505b28aaff3aa0b23debd2ec6172a72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"62\" height=\"20\" /> is integral for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/7/0079bac059bdeba8f6214fbdbc311b2b1f4a0d4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"67\" height=\"16\" />. To start, we would like to find a recurrence relation for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/e/1be728aed505b28aaff3aa0b23debd2ec6172a72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"62\" height=\"20\" />. First, let's look at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/2/b526dd8250cf12f4f218f59894d2e2d518be83b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,n-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/1/a5129f68d4e20ec9e0102d10e65e4cc422defcd0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}     f(m,n-1) &amp;=\\frac{(2m)!(2n-2)!}{m!(n-1)!(m+n-1)!}\\\\      &amp;=\\frac{(2m)!(2n)!(n-1)(m+n)}{m!n!(m+n)!(2n-1)(2n-2)}\\\\      &amp;=f(m,n) \\cdot \\frac{(n-1)(m+n)}{(2n-1)(2n-2)}\\\\      &amp;=f(m,n) \\cdot \\frac{m+n}{2(2n-1)} \\end{align*}\" width=\"353\" height=\"188\" />\nSecond, let's look at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/7/7c7e95ebaa41ced4689a4d7733c919255dc96c5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m+1,n-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"18\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/8/5e885920cbcff41c13c0c9ded8f8977ea47621e3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}     f(m+1,n-1) &amp;=\\frac{(2m+2)!(2n-2)!}{(m+1)!(n-1)!(m+n)!}\\\\     &amp;=\\frac{(2m)!(2n)!(2m+1)(2m+2)(n-1)}{m!n!(m+n)!(m+1)(2n-1)(2n-2)}\\\\     &amp;= f(m,n) \\cdot  \\frac{(2m+1)(2m+2)(n-1)}{(m+1)(2n-1)(2n-2)}\\\\     &amp;=f(m,n) \\cdot \\frac{(2m+1)}{2n-1} \\end{align*}\" width=\"446\" height=\"187\" />\nCombining,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c7238e0c819ce8d99acac7898beb67168e8bb0d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}     4f(m,n-1)-f(m+1,n-1) &amp;=f(m,n)\\cdot \\Bigg(\\frac{4(m+n)}{2(2n-1)}-\\frac{2m+1}{2n-1}\\Bigg)\\\\     &amp;=f(m,n) \\cdot \\frac{2m+2n-2m-1}{2n-1}\\\\     &amp;=f(m,n). \\end{align*}\" width=\"527\" height=\"124\" />\nTherefore, we have found the recurrence relation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/e/b4ed31cf01181f86d638c78628e8152c61605145.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(m,n)=4f(m,n-1)-f(m+1,n-1).\\]\" width=\"332\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/8/9e847a278771d813adac52dd28057cbe3cb8d31a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" /> is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/6/686c554076afb1731731eed56cff474fad84ec6d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{2m}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"49\" height=\"53\" />, which is an integer for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/2/cc2af59abb37cd00ec34380ab1c9aed5316b08c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" />. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/7/3c764090157f8b009f26384941dc177d0feda8ce.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(m,1)=4f(m,0)-f(m+1,0),\\]\" width=\"264\" height=\"18\" /> so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/4/dc411370a14774a038c465a4ab477fbbd33c8019.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> is an integer, and therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/e/30e7b0a7df2eca90894332c362836bd941ae4fe9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,2)=4f(m,1)-f(m+1,1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"259\" height=\"18\" /> must be an integer, etc. \n</p><p>By induction, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/e/1be728aed505b28aaff3aa0b23debd2ec6172a72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"62\" height=\"20\" /> is an integer for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/7/0079bac059bdeba8f6214fbdbc311b2b1f4a0d4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n \\geq 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"67\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>Borrowed from <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~asdas/imo/imo/isoln/isoln723.html\">http://www.cs.cornell.edu/~asdas/imo/imo/isoln/isoln723.html</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let p be a prime, and n be an integer. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/a/31af5a838c72fb94c5413a7f82bc4194f7a5ff9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_p(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> be the largest positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/0/3304f8ae0984919cfe89f0af53cdd3479f98d3c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^k|n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>WTS: For all primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/2/6a24ba24cffe2353edead8632478e64e5d8a1ac7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$V_p((2m)!)+V_p((2n)!) \\ge V_p(m!)+V_p(n!)+V_p((m+n)!)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"440\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/0/28086d023ffec8b300001723f01650fdc52f1298.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[V_p(x!)=\\sum_{a=1}^{\\infty} \\left\\lfloor{\\frac{x}{p^a}}\\right\\rfloor\\]\" width=\"137\" height=\"48\" />\n</p><p>Lemma 2.1: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/b/e9bdf3e496517e8f8742f81dd774be1f93cf6e8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> be real numbers. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/3/d737333f1e4fbc8b994e69618a1da34f38093664.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor{2a}\\rfloor+\\lfloor{2b}\\rfloor\\ge\\lfloor{a}\\rfloor+\\lfloor{b}\\rfloor+\\lfloor{a+b}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"261\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>Proof of Lemma 2.1: Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/d/90d64cf5788d9567ab4e6425341f104abae89e68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1=\\lfloor{a}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"64\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/2/01284e73394ea1f58d9cdb6fde9f1545b13e2119.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_1=\\lfloor{b}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/3/4931ffd4c8af454aa9f044eb786dfab7955ff8c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor{2a}\\rfloor+\\lfloor{2b}\\rfloor=2(a_1+b_1)+\\lfloor2\\{a\\}\\rfloor+\\lfloor2\\{b\\}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"337\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>On the other hand, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/c/7fcbcc8827fcf6d338d73dff3337187a7fe6f075.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor{a}\\rfloor+\\lfloor{b}\\rfloor+\\lfloor{a+b}\\rfloor=a_1+b_1+(a_1+b_1)+\\lfloor\\{2(a+b)\\}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"436\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>It is trivial that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/a/eca557a1e030ac9c9fe8eeff1efb2ec95dfebf71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lfloor2\\{a\\}\\rfloor+\\lfloor2\\{b\\}\\rfloor\\ge\\lfloor\\{2(a+b)\\}\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"245\" height=\"19\" /> (Triangle Inequality)\n</p><p>Apply Lemma 2.1 to the problem: and we are pretty much done.\n</p><p>Note: I am lazy, so this is only the most important part. I hope you can come up with the rest of the solution. This is my work, but perhaps someone have come up with this method before I did.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remark_about.2C_and_correction_to_Solution_2\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark_about,_and_correction_to_Solution_2\">Remark about, and correction to Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remark about, and correction to Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The last step seems mistaken. For example [1.5]+[1.5]&lt;[1.5+1.5]. Triangle inequality is about absolute value |x| not floor function [x]. Following is a rectified solution.\n</p><p>Lemma: [a+b]&lt;=[a]+[b]+1, a,b&gt;=0.\n</p><p>Proof: a&lt;[a]+1, b&lt;[b]+1, so a+b&lt;[a]+[b]+2, [a+b]&lt;=a+b&lt;[a]+[b]+2, so [a+b]&lt;=[a]+[b]+1.\n</p><p>Let (x,2x,…,nx)=n, p is any prime,\nV2(9!)=V2(1•2•3•4•5•6•7•8•9)\n=(2,4,6,8)+(4,8)+(8)=[9/2]+[9/4]+[9/8]=7.\n</p><p>Generalize it and we have,\nVp(m!)=Vp(1•2•….•m)=[m/p]+[m/p^2]+…+[m/p^k], k=1~∞, or,\n</p><p>Vp(m!)=∑([m/p^k],k=1~∞)<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/0/220a2f95ee2f18fd0dd175c20727cde4a7dcfffb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ \\ \\ \\ (1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Obviously when p^k&gt;m, k&gt;=1, [m/p^k]=0.\n</p><p>Note the target statement is equivalent to:\n</p><p>for any prime p,\nVp((2m)!(2n)!)&gt;=Vp(m!n!(m+n)!), or,\n</p><p>Vp((2m)!)+Vp((2n)!)&gt;=Vp(m!)+Vp(n!)+Vp(m+n)! <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/1/231fb5f143ab0f7acb9081da2b1523c003df86c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ \\ \\ \\ (2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Let m/p^k=a(k), n/p^k=b(k), k=1~∞.\nCombine (1), we note that (2) is equivalent to:\n</p><p>for any k in (1), [2a(k)]+[2b(k)]&gt;=[a(k)]+[b(k)]+[a(k)+b(k)] <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/6/d76ff4bd1f422865fb159c84e0ab79db0f26865c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ \\ \\ \\ (3)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>For convenience of viewing, let a(k)=a, b(k)=b, a,b&gt;=0, (3) is then rewritten as:\n</p><p>[2a]+[2b]&gt;=[a]+[b]+[a+b] <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/2/8727db3203f8bd273a1d9fe6cfe0f5fa7084dd5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ \\ \\ \\ (4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>We prove it under different scenarios:\n</p><p>-If a&lt;[a]+1/2, b&lt;[b]+1/2,\nThen 2[a]&lt;=[2a]&lt;=2a&lt;2[a]+1, [2a]=2[a], likewise, [2b]=2[b], so (4) is equivalent to [a]+[b]&gt;=[a+b]. Per current condition, a+b&lt;[a]+[b]+1, so [a+b]&lt;[[a]+[b]+1]=[a]+[b]+1, also obviously [a+b]&gt;=[a]+[b], so [a+b]=[a]+[b], proved;\n</p><p>-If a&lt;[a]+1/2, b&gt;=[b]+1/2, then [2a]=2[a], [2b]=2[b]+1, so (4) is equivalent to [a]+[b]+1&gt;=[a+b], as per lemma, proved;\n</p><p>-If a&gt;=[a]+1/2, b&gt;=[b]+1/2, so [2a]=2[a]+1, [2b]=2[b]+1, so (4) is equivalent to [a]+[b]+2&gt;=[a+b], as per lemma, proved;\n</p><p>Therefore (4) is proved, so is (2), as every step from (2) to (4) is reversible. Therefore (2m)!(2n)!/(m!n!(m+n)!) is an integer. Proof completed.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remarks_.28added_by_pf02.2C_April_2025.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_April_2025)\">Remarks (added by pf02, April 2025)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Remarks (added by pf02, April 2025)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>1. The first solution has a few errors in the computation,\nbut they cancel out, and the proof is essentially correct.\nBelow I will give the corrections, to make the solution\nflawless.\n</p><p>2. As pointed out by a contributor, the second solution is\nincorrect.  The correction given is good, but very sloppily\nwritten and hard to read (even after I edited the spacing,\nto make it legible).  Below, I will repeat the argument in\na simplified form, to make it easier to follow.\n</p><p>3. I will give a third solution below, inspired by the\npapers mentioned in the next paragraph.\n</p><p>4. The problem is a classical result, attributed to Eugene\nCatalan, who stated it in a paper published in 1874.  The\nnumbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/9/4f971ea987cd060b455f7f1717b8cbaf6e3cbd99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(m, n) = \\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{m!n!(m+n)!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"197\" height=\"45\" /> are\nsometimes called the super-Catalan numbers.  (They are\nmentioned at the end of\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catalan_number\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catalan_number</a>).\n</p><p>There are a few papers published about them.  See for example\nthe paper \"The Super Catalan Numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/2/dd2bdc8889c69e52960895b8f637c9e82b7a76df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(m, m + s)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/a/3ca420b71632f6785115941dc43bd619fc5d9d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s \\le 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"44\" height=\"16\" />\nand Some Integer Factorial Ratios\" by Xin Chen &amp; Jane Wang\n(<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~reiner/REU/ChenWang2012.pdf\">https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~reiner/REU/ChenWang2012.pdf</a>),\nand \"Super Ballot Numbers\" by Ira M. Gessel (especially section 6)\n(<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0747717192900342/pdf\">https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0747717192900342/pdf</a>).\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Corrected_computations_in_Solution_1\">Corrected computations in Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Corrected computations in Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We have\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/e/62ef0979595ed6e3a060715e81e157b7b0b3bc67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m,n-1) = \\frac{(2m)!(2n-2)!}{m!(n-1)!(m+n-1)!} = \\frac{(2m)!(2n)! \\cdot (n)(m+n)}{m!n!(m+n)! \\cdot (2n-1)(2n)} =  f(m,n) \\cdot \\frac{m+n}{2(2n-1)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"725\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>and\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/6/6567b3fdfdc3cc6354bd12844cb67cb44edf7b74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(m+1,n-1) = \\frac{(2m+2)!(2n-2)!}{(m+1)!(n-1)!(m+n)!} = \\frac{(2m)!(2n)! \\cdot (2m+1)(2m+2)(n)}{m!n!(m+n)! \\cdot (m+1)(2n-1)(2n)} = f(m,n) \\cdot \\frac{2m+1}{2n-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"814\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>The rest of the proof is correct.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Simplified_version_of_the_correction_to_Solution_2\">Simplified version of the correction to Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Simplified version of the correction to Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/9/4f971ea987cd060b455f7f1717b8cbaf6e3cbd99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(m, n) = \\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{m!n!(m+n)!}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"197\" height=\"45\" /> is an\ninteger, we have to prove that for any prime number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, the\nexponent of the largest power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/d/9bdfd3b267ae7a87f5f994582cb98ba9774c2b9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2m)!(2n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" /> is larger\nthan the exponent of the largest power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/f/34f6f8d654cde05d67991ecd715213e32c654310.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m!n!(m+n)!$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"107\" height=\"20\" />.\n(In fact, we have to consider only primes which satisfy\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/6/5b67f8d2a6a562eb11b75069056580b74a3a17bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\le m + n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"17\" />).\n</p><p>The largest power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/9/fc97ef67268cd4e91bacdf12b8901d7036c9a056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$N$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" />! is given by\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/7186c48c2a49c35730a35b61830840c4657ef438.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\nu_p(N!) = \\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} \\left\\lfloor\\frac{N}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"149\" height=\"53\" />.\n(The sum is a finite sum, since we have to consider only powers\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/e/b1e9a47db333404d4a561513b5ca3b0fbc2cb3bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i \\le \\lfloor\\log_p N\\rfloor.)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"111\" height=\"22\" />  This is easy to see, but it\nis a reasonably well known fact (see <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Legendre%27s_Formula\" title=\"Legendre&#39;s Formula\">Legendre's Formula</a> or\n<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legendre&#39;s_formula\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Legendre&#39;s_formula</a>.)  Note that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b67ad6e99db440e3532eab3e1105bdbc8ffa8e1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\nu_p(M!N!) = \\nu_p(M!) + \\nu_p(N!)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"233\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Thus, it is enough to prove that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/0/e00e15ebc4b1e8d558b7f377f3d92cae80a3f264.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor\\frac{2n}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor \\ge \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m + n}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor\\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ \\ (1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"412\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/7/05751327d98ee78b2a493114c4dd534f1a6520ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m = Q_1 \\cdot p^i + R_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"21\" /> be the integer division of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/6/5961a34f1d146caddb87089f240db3fdef4fb421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"21\" />\n(so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/8/c38e370e47aaecf477ee402d6ae5e68909af0a11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\le Q_1 &lt; p^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"96\" height=\"21\" />), and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c7f7d0cdc9cabd5bbc4084c7478bc89f55ab065.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 = \\frac{Q_1}{p^i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"68\" height=\"43\" />. In other words,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/c/03cfc047e6df7e35eeb293cbdbb45c1e1b3d0375.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 = \\frac{m}{p^i} - \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"125\" height=\"45\" />.\nNote that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/4/504c5adcc4fe2a34518f79587290a8cd6b49cd15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 \\le r_1 &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"17\" />.  Similarly, obtain <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b4f3f4b698ce35667432041e168f7653533fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> from the division\nof <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/6/5961a34f1d146caddb87089f240db3fdef4fb421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>Note that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/5/a45677f0e96912c2a6d727cf1d4d0e67aa1e581e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor = 2\\left\\lfloor\\frac{m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"129\" height=\"45\" />\nor\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/0/370775b87104dd5e374cf8e2717d97ac6c887990.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor = 2\\left\\lfloor\\frac{m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"160\" height=\"45\" />\ndepending on whether <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/c/43c4e4195b0cd8656d02e62206e215e3f85c3d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 &lt; \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"38\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/b/66bd86b0fa5fc86b4f4844605c7d432ee17fdef5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 \\ge \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"38\" /> and similarly\nfor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/4/0b4f3f4b698ce35667432041e168f7653533fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" />.  Also\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/9/009584e0c98397bcfb2579538c4dc59e6ec78389.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{m + n}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"202\" height=\"45\" />\nor\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/d/9ed667b8d533f2cd4170cc6f4200f2a4c64c42ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left\\lfloor\\frac{m + n}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor\\frac{m}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor\\frac{n}{p^i}\\right\\rfloor + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"233\" height=\"45\" />\ndepending on whether <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/b/e1b7a1a6285c8aa42e064faabc304556b0172101.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 + r_2 &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/c/66cb40d4d8f8c331c4cba65fd377bb194f6664b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 + r_2 \\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p><p>We have the following cases:\n</p><p>1. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/c/43c4e4195b0cd8656d02e62206e215e3f85c3d73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 &lt; \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"38\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e65af9e895e677bf4b6c72861710928d453f34c8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_2 &lt; \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"55\" height=\"38\" /> then we have\nequality in (1).\n</p><p>2. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb4314859931b03577b6d5ef37777279cb0ade6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 \\ge \\frac{1}{2}, r_2 &lt; \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"116\" height=\"38\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/b/e1b7a1a6285c8aa42e064faabc304556b0172101.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 + r_2 &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" />\nthen after cancelling terms, the left hand side in (1) becomes 1, and\nthe right hand side becomes 0.\n</p><p>3. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/4/eb4314859931b03577b6d5ef37777279cb0ade6c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 \\ge \\frac{1}{2}, r_2 &lt; \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"116\" height=\"38\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/c/66cb40d4d8f8c331c4cba65fd377bb194f6664b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 + r_2 \\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"17\" />\nthen we have equality in (1).\n</p><p>4, 5. The two cases when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf4abb6e9485cd27c7f70add58938718864eeea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 &lt; \\frac{1}{2}, r_2 \\ge \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"116\" height=\"38\" />\nare similar to cases 2 and 3.\n</p><p>6. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/7/f676fe0121b3d37f1c77bd8234641a0d05af1d8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r_1 \\ge \\frac{1}{2}, r_2 \\ge \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"116\" height=\"38\" />, then after cancelling\nterms, the left hand side in (1) becomes 2, and the right hand side\nbecomes 1.\n</p><p>The above shows that (1) is always true, which proves the problem.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>This solution uses the fact that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/b/38b9c3469dc5297eaea48f193abe73b0816590ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n - k)!}, k = 0, \\dots n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"241\" height=\"55\" /> are the coefficients\nof the powers of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> in the expansion of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/6/0b663dfb520c034978ce0a4f292ce720852f47d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1 + x)^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"20\" /> (see <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Binomial_Theorem\" title=\"Binomial Theorem\">Binomial Theorem</a>\nor <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binomial_coefficient</a>).  Of course these\ncoefficients, hence these expressions in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/f/3cff91106986d596b444b21427ff9ee5ac08db4c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n, k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"28\" height=\"16\" /> are integers.\n</p><p>Without loss of generality, we can assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/e/5deb651b747daf94891c9213a616a1c1de03bfc1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m \\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"50\" height=\"14\" />.  We will use the identity\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/7/ff720e2d1c59d022a848db909faace0aac21be37.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(m, n) = \\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{m!n!(m+n)!} = (-1)^m \\sum_{k=0}^{k=2m} (-1)^k {2m \\choose k} {2n \\choose m + n - k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"518\" height=\"56\" />.\n</p><p>This identity is attributed to K. von Szily (1894).  Note that we could\ntake the sum over all integers, using the convention that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/a/d8ad0b9baf46cf2acdc5bfea0c70e321cc6e28a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${n \\choose k} = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"74\" height=\"55\" />\nwhen <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/3/b634700c95ed9caa5d7bf32c919a89a97dc9a1a8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &lt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/2/30236402777f499778852128a0242a5ee88f0f7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k &gt; n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"45\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>The fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/9/5795f112024e51560ea20824593328e39847bb5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S(m, n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"20\" /> is integer follows immediately from this identity\nbecause the right hand side is integer.  \n</p><p>Thus, we just need to prove this identity.\n</p><p>Start with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d80fdc3ca67e4a0af5ade717dcc14b85103ddf1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1 - x^2)^{m + n} = (1 + x)^{m + n} (1 - x)^{m + n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"294\" height=\"21\" />.  Expand\nboth sides and look at the coefficient of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/3/a93be219cb11dd4ee6ccfabcab9b31b74cc2c024.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"17\" /> in both expansions.\nEquate the two expressions giving the coefficient of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/3/a93be219cb11dd4ee6ccfabcab9b31b74cc2c024.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{2m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"17\" /> in the two\nsides.  We get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/7/897e5592ec414c9e877d97e09523fcee76746c58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-1)^m {m + n \\choose m} = \\sum_{k=0}^{2m} {m + n \\choose k} \\cdot (-1)^{2m - k} {m + n \\choose 2m - k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"432\" height=\"56\" />.\n</p><p>Write out the binomial coefficients in terms of factorials, and multiply\nboth sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/7/3d71c84256d49f7dd4d57f460e6514d8d8b195ba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-1)^m \\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{[(m + n)!]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"142\" height=\"45\" />.  We get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/7/237b91f3295159193b04b942ff0ea6d0f10ae04e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(m + n)!}{m!n!} \\cdot \\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{[(m + n)!]^2} = (-1)^m \\sum_{k=0}^{2m} (-1)^k \\frac{(m + n)!}{k!(m + n - k)!} \\cdot \\frac{(m + n)!}{(2m - k)!(n - m + k)!} \\cdot \\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{[(m + n)!]^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"742\" height=\"56\" />\n</p><p>Simplifying and rearranging factors, we get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0619644465898ddf907278545e0782c9b031e7aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(2m)!(2n)!}{m!n!(m + n)!} = (-1)^m \\sum_{k=0}^{2m} (-1)^k \\frac{(2m)!}{k!(2m - k)!} \\cdot \\frac{(2n)!}{(n + m - k)!(n - m + k)!} = (-1)^m \\sum_{k=0}^{2m} (-1)^k {2m \\choose k} {2n \\choose n + m - k}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"884\" height=\"56\" />\n</p><p>This finishes the problem.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO\" title=\"1972 IMO\">1972 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1972_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1972 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017171046\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.058 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.078 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 571/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1229/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 21285/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   12.464      1 -total\n 57.14%    7.122      1 Template:IMO_box\n 40.32%    5.025      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:12088:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017171046 and revision id 247454. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}